<%#
  How the savings figures are worked out, in the chef's own terms.

  This exists so nobody -- staff or chef -- has to reverse-engineer the method
  from the number. Every claim on the reports pages traces back to the rules
  stated here, and if a figure ever contradicts one of them, that is a bug.
%>
<details class="group border-t border-gray-200">
  <summary class="flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 cursor-pointer text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-gray-900 hover:bg-gray-50 select-none">
    <svg class="w-4 h-4 shrink-0 transition-transform group-open:rotate-90" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true">
      <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7" />
    </svg>
    <span class="font-medium">How we work this out</span>
  </summary>

  <div class="px-6 pb-6 pt-1 text-sm text-gray-600 space-y-5 max-w-3xl">
    <div>
      <p class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">The two numbers</p>
      <p>
        For every line you ordered, we look at what the same item costs at your other
        suppliers and compare it to what you actually paid.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-2 space-y-1.5 list-disc list-inside">
        <li><span class="font-medium text-gray-900">Saved</span> — what you beat the most expensive supplier by.</li>
        <li><span class="font-medium text-gray-900">Left on the table</span> — what you'd have saved buying from the cheapest.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-2">
        Buy the cheapest and you left nothing behind. Buy the dearest and you saved nothing.
        Buy something in the middle and both are true at once — the two always add up to the
        full price spread on that item.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Everything is priced per unit first</p>
      <p>
        Suppliers quote differently — a case price from one, a price per pound from another.
        A 40&nbsp;lb case at $2.50/lb is $100, not $2.50. We convert every price to the same
        unit before comparing, then multiply by the amount you actually bought.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">We only compare like with like</p>
      <p>A line is left out of the numbers entirely when:</p>
      <ul class="mt-2 space-y-1.5 list-disc list-inside">
        <li>no other supplier carries anything matched to it</li>
        <li>the only alternatives come in a very different pack — a single pound is not a substitute for a 36&nbsp;lb case</li>
        <li>the cheapest and dearest are so far apart they can't be the same product</li>
        <li>the pack size can't be read well enough to be sure</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-2">
        Left out means <span class="font-medium text-gray-900">not counted</span>, not counted as zero.
        We would rather show a smaller number we can stand behind than a bigger one we can't.
      </p>
    </div>

    <% if defined?(compared_lines) && compared_lines.to_i.positive? %>
      <div class="rounded-lg bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 px-4 py-3">
        <p class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">What this covers</p>
        <p>
          <%= number_with_delimiter(compared_lines) %> of
          <%= number_with_delimiter(total_lines) %> order lines had an alternative we could
          compare<% if defined?(compared_spend) && compared_spend.to_f.positive? %>,
          covering <%= number_to_currency(compared_spend) %> of
          <%= number_to_currency(total_spend) %> spent<% end %>.
          The rest is either single-source or not matched yet, so no saving is claimed on it either way.
        </p>
      </div>
    <% end %>

    <div>
      <p class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Where the comparisons come from</p>
      <p>
        Three places: items your team matched by hand, items linked to the same product across
        suppliers, and automatic matches we make across the products you actually buy. Anything
        your team has rejected is never used.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</details>
